<!-- ======= SECTION 6: KEY TAKEAWAYS ======= -->
<div class="report-section" id="section-takeaways">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-tag">Section 06</div>
      <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</h2>
      <p>Synthesized findings for research policy analysts and funding strategists. Five prioritized insights, each paired with a direct strategic implication.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway-list">
      <div class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num">1</div>
        <div>
          <div class="takeaway-title">Open Access is the New Default — But Adoption is Uneven</div>
          <div class="takeaway-body">
            65.1% of all indexed publications in this dataset are open access, and 2025 shows the rate climbing to 74.1% — a 10 percentage-point jump over 2024. The three highest-volume journals (Scientific Reports, Heliyon, Cureus) are all open-access mega-journals, reflecting a structural shift in biomedical publishing norms. However, China — the world's top biomedical output nation in this dataset — has an OA rate of only 55.5%, substantially below peers like the USA (76.4%), UK (80.8%), and Iran (86.9%).
          </div>
          <div class="takeaway-implication">Fund targeted OA compliance support and incentive programs in China-affiliated institutions to close the 20+ point OA gap. Engage with Chinese national research councils on OA mandate alignment.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num">2</div>
        <div>
          <div class="takeaway-title">China Dominates Output Volume; India Shows the Fastest Growth Trajectory</div>
          <div class="takeaway-body">
            China accounts for 22.1% of all publications (1,082 records), more than double the USA's 10.7% (521 records). The top 10 countries collectively represent only 52.4% of total output, indicating a genuinely global research base. Notably, India's 2025 publication rate relative to its 2024 baseline suggests accelerating output — with 59 records in 2025 vs. 129 in 2024, India is outpacing expectations for an incomplete-indexing year and may be approaching a top-3 position in the coming 2–3 years.
          </div>
          <div class="takeaway-implication">Prioritize research partnership frameworks and co-funding mechanisms with Indian institutions. Monitor India's publishing trajectory in the next 12–18 months as a leading indicator of a shift in the global biomedical research center of gravity.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num">3</div>
        <div>
          <div class="takeaway-title">COVID-19 Research Has Peaked; Oncology and Neurology Are the New Anchors</div>
          <div class="takeaway-body">
            COVID-19 remains the #1 single major topic in the dataset with 43 records, but fell from 37 in 2024 to just 5 in 2025 — an 86% decline — signaling the end of the pandemic research surge. Oncology-related topics (breast cancer, hepatocellular carcinoma, stomach cancer, renal cell carcinoma, neoplasms combined) and neurological conditions (Alzheimer, Parkinson, Autism Spectrum Disorder) show stable multi-year presence and together represent the most durable research focus areas.
          </div>
          <div class="takeaway-implication">Reallocate pandemic-era emergency funding into durable oncology and neurological disease research programs. Consider the microbiome (16 major-topic records with consistent 2024–2026 presence) as an emerging priority area for targeted investment.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num">4</div>
        <div>
          <div class="takeaway-title">Preprints Signal Emerging High-Collaboration Research — Watch This Cohort</div>
          <div class="takeaway-body">
            Preprints (41 records, 0.8% of the dataset) have the highest author collaboration signature of any publication type: median 9 authors, mean 12.1. In biomedical science, preprints increasingly precede high-impact journal publications. The presence of bioRxiv as the 7th-largest indexed source (32 records) reinforces a growing preprint culture. The gap between preprint median (9) and standard journal article median (6) suggests preprints disproportionately represent large consortium-level science.
          </div>
          <div class="takeaway-implication">Develop preprint monitoring pipelines as an early-warning system for emerging research directions. Consider funding mechanisms that explicitly support large-consortium preprint-first projects, particularly in fast-moving areas like AI applications in biomedicine.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num">5</div>
        <div>
          <div class="takeaway-title">Early 2026 Indexing Rates Suggest Sustained or Growing Research Output</div>
          <div class="takeaway-body">
            The 2026 monthly publication counts show a clear upward trend: January (51) → February (76) → March (98). This is a higher trajectory than the equivalent first-quarter months of 2025 (January: 30, February: 52, March: 56). While indexing lag limits direct year-over-year comparison, the early 2026 pattern is consistent with a sustained or modestly growing global biomedical research output base — despite potential funding pressures in some national research systems.
          </div>
          <div class="takeaway-implication">Plan for continued growth in indexed biomedical literature through at least 2027. Infrastructure investment decisions (databases, literature synthesis tools, reviewer capacity) should be sized for a growing publication base, not a plateau.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

